const CONTENT={"en": [{"title": "Technical Skills", "body": "<ul><li><strong>AI systems and project tools:</strong> Multi-agent orchestration, Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI, MCP, model routing, context management, Python, PostgreSQL, and Godot 4.</li><li><strong>Languages:</strong> Go, TypeScript, JavaScript, SQL, AQL, shell scripting; Java for Android development.</li><li><strong>Backend and architecture:</strong> WebSockets, REST APIs, gRPC, microservices, distributed systems, high availability, asynchronous I/O, concurrency, dependency injection, and inversion of control.</li><li><strong>Data and messaging:</strong> ArangoDB, Redis Cluster, MySQL, Microsoft SQL Server, NATS, and JetStream.</li><li><strong>Delivery and testing:</strong> Docker, Jenkins, Git, Nginx, PM2, Mocha, Dredd, Newman, Swagger, Jira, PlantUML, and Markdown.</li><li><strong>Domain experience:</strong> Gaming platforms, casino management systems, wallet services, betting records, return-to-player (RTP) management, and risk controls.</li></ul><p><strong>Additional skills from the original résumé:</strong> Algorithms, data structures, Epoll/Reactor, multithreading, multiprocessing, coroutines, web server/application development and game servers.</p>"}, {"title": "Current Independent Project", "body": "<h3>Independent Game Project — Developer and AI Systems Designer</h3><p>September 2026–Present | Godot 4 survival game in development</p><p>Design and direct a multi-agent development system for a third-person post-apocalyptic survival game. Own architecture, work allocation, review criteria, acceptance decisions, and iteration priorities; AI agents write most of the code. The game has not been released.</p><h4>Architecture: treating AI collaboration as an engineering team</h4><ul><li>Designed three persistent Claude roles covering process and cost, content-production dispatch, and servers/game systems, alongside three Codex execution accounts and Gemini CLI. Separated decision-making, implementation, and review responsibilities; evolved the architecture through more than five iterations in a month from one coordinating conversation to delegated roles.</li><li>Configured up to 15 concurrent conversations per Codex account. This is configured capacity, not sustained utilization or verified throughput. Personally owned architecture, operating rules, acceptance criteria, and decisions; AI agents wrote most of the code.</li></ul><h4>End-to-end dispatch and traceable delivery</h4><ul><li>Decomposed goals into initiatives, acceptance-ready work packages, and execution tasks (G/M/W). A unified dispatch entry point selects account, model, and reasoning level from available quota and task difficulty, links each dispatch to its parent requirement, and creates execution records.</li><li>Designed a dispatch → initial check → independent review → installation → repeated verification → status-update workflow. Database-backed progress views separate decisions requiring human judgment. The November 3, 2026 snapshot lists 31 initiatives and 401 work packages, with 197 marked complete; approximately 2,100 Codex dispatches logged since October 27 measure activity, not completed requirements.</li></ul><h4>Go orchestration service and incremental migration</h4><ul><li>Led AI-assisted development of a single Go orchestration service to progressively consolidate hundreds of persistent Python scripts. Modules cover event-hook dispatch, Codex app-server conversation control, dispatch state machines, mailboxes, scheduling, health checks, subprocess supervision, and memory limits.</li><li>Captured legacy inputs and outputs as golden samples, compared Go and Python behavior, and used a three-stage rollout with rollback paths. Project statistics report 35 internal packages, approximately 131,000 non-test lines, and 410 test files. The core migration has 48 work packages marked complete; remaining scripts are still being migrated. Code volume and test-file counts are not coverage or pass-rate measures.</li></ul><h4>Inter-agent communication and work continuity</h4><ul><li>Replaced file-based messages with PostgreSQL role mailboxes, adding receipt monitoring, uncollected-message alerts, and wake-up handling. Reserved a fallback channel for urgent work and outages to reduce interruptions. Logged tool calls and failures for later investigation.</li><li>Designed automatic context compression, rolling handoff documents, and A/B handovers for primary roles, preserving task IDs, file locations, and pending responses. Database checks prevent two primary instances from becoming active simultaneously. Documented recovery of applications, database services, schedules, and mailboxes after reboot; long-running operation is a design goal, not a verified availability SLA.</li></ul><h4>Quality gates, failure handling, and acceptance</h4><ul><li>Used Gemini for an initial read-only check with spot verification, followed by independent Claude Opus and Codex reviews. Integration requires both reviews and self-tests to pass. Later review rounds focus on major issues, with simplified alternatives escalated when work repeatedly fails review.</li><li>Designed detection, interruption, and restart handling for stalled execution; repeated errors trigger a different approach on the third occurrence. Pre/post-tool hooks constrain role responsibilities, scanning scope, and test behavior. Periodic maintenance turns newly discovered failure modes into future checks.</li><li>Queued Godot tests with resource prechecks and headless automation, while requiring server-connected conditions for performance measurement. Headless success is not visual acceptance. Character animation, the server-connected game build, and the 60 FPS target remain in progress.</li></ul><h4>Data-informed model routing and quota management</h4><ul><li>Analyzed account-quota changes across 541 dispatches, estimating roughly 6.2%, 1.1%, and 0.2% of weekly quota per task for high-, upper-mid-, and mid-tier models. These are order-of-magnitude comparisons: concurrent work can be counted more than once, so they are not precise per-task token or billing costs.</li><li>Reserved high-tier models for first-of-a-kind difficult work, reused review conversations, and routed small changes to lighter models. Non-LLM scripts select full-speed, normal, or economy modes from remaining quota and reset timing, with low-quota pause, handoff, and recovery handling. A 70–80% reduction in high-tier usage is projected, not measured savings or a throughput guarantee.</li></ul><h4>Knowledge engineering and AI content pipelines</h4><ul><li>Built a three-layer rules index → topic files → evidence structure, more than ten topic knowledge bases, 47 Claude skills, approximately 90 project rules, and vector memory. Converted recurring dispatch, review, inventory, and handoff work into reusable procedures.</li><li>Integrated NVIDIA Kimodo motion generation and standard skeletons, MotionBricks transition baking, Tripo 3D, Blender MCP, ComfyUI, Gemini image generation, and ElevenLabs audio. Defined machine-checkable standards for polygon counts, UVs, open edges, and color palettes.</li><li>Project records report review and triage of approximately 850 environment textures and 307 sound assets, alongside building dimensions, monster inventories, and game-balance validation specifications. Reviewed assets do not imply that all assets are shipped or the game is complete.</li></ul><h3>Judgment and execution: from constraints to the next validation</h3><h4>Diagnose the constraint before scaling collaboration | Mid-September–early October</h4><ul><li>Started with one Claude coordinator and one Codex implementer exchanging Markdown messages. As files grew, repeated reading consumed more context and new conversations lost continuity. Identified information retention and handoff design, not just model capability, as the constraint; bounded open messages and file size before redesigning communication.</li></ul><h4>Separate knowledge access and responsibility | October 11–20</h4><ul><li>Reorganized scattered rules into an index → topic files → evidence hierarchy and converted recurring mistakes into reusable procedures. When initial checks produced unreliable evidence, limited that role to read-only work and spot-checked its output rather than treating model statements as verification.</li><li>A single conversation handling dispatch, review, and process control created context pressure and self-review risk. Split these responsibilities across three persistent roles. Project records describe two successful A/B handover tests; a later failure exposed lost in-flight subagent work, prompting a requirement to deliver or hand off before clearing a conversation, enforced with a hook.</li></ul><h4>Replace reminders with traceable coordination | October 22–25</h4><ul><li>Replaced fragile file mailboxes and direct messaging calls with background delivery and PostgreSQL mailboxes, including receipt tracking and uncollected-message alerts. Stopped idle backup conversations from waiting continuously for mail and documented reboot recovery, balancing continuity against quota consumption.</li></ul><h4>Instrument first, then optimize | October 27–30</h4><ul><li>Recorded dispatches in a database before comparing execution and usage. Replaced a costly, slow multi-seat art voting process with author self-checks and an initial check, while retaining independent cross-review for code integration. Converted approved plans into owned tasks immediately instead of leaving them only in documents.</li></ul><h4>Shrink the rollout unit after failures | October 31–November 3</h4><ul><li>A growing fleet of persistent Python scripts increased memory pressure and debugging cost. Led consolidation into a Go service using captured legacy inputs/outputs, behavioral comparisons, and staged rollback. Project records describe repeated installation failures, including memory exhaustion, followed by fallback to the previous implementation.</li><li>Adjusted execution to migrate one script at a time, observe it for ten minutes, and retain the old implementation only where problems remained. Prioritized reversible changes over a single all-at-once cutover. These are source-reported recovery experiences, not independently verified zero-downtime or production-availability claims.</li></ul><h4>Let tests override review opinions and bound review loops</h4><ul><li>A review had judged a change unaffected, but subsequent tests failed in three areas. Prioritized actual runtime results and post-write readback over model assertions; used real screenshots for UI decisions and required server-connected performance measurements. Headless tests do not establish visual acceptance.</li><li>One work item accumulated as many as 25 review tickets. Introduced a major-issues-only scope from round three and escalation of a simpler alternative when review continued to fail. Repeated errors trigger a different approach; unnecessary abstractions are removed to keep effort focused on verifiable core functionality.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Source scope: based on the author’s project record updated November 3, 2026. Statistics are source-reported; the database, source code, and test evidence were not independently re-audited. The game remains in development; these workflows and figures do not establish shipped-product reliability or performance.</p>"}, {"title": "Current Professional Case Study: AI-Assisted Backend Engineering", "body": "<h3>Existing Online-Game Platform — AI-Assisted Backend Workflow</h3><p>Late July 2026–Present | Anonymized professional case study</p><p>Owned workflow design, boundary decisions, and the organization of repair and validation; AI participated in investigation, implementation, and testing. This describes personal decisions and iterations, not sole authorship of all code or acceptance of every repair in production.</p><h4>Map the system before allowing changes</h4><ul><li>Since late July 2026, designed AI-assisted investigation, repair, local testing, and development-environment deployment workflows for an existing online-game platform. Used actual build and deployment configuration to distinguish active services from historical repositories. Required checks of remotes, versions, and uncommitted changes before work on isolated branches.</li></ul><h4>Load knowledge by task and separate discovery from repair</h4><ul><li>Repeatedly loading large rule sets increased context overhead. Introduced task-to-document routing and role-specific entry documents. Separated read-only investigation from repair decisions, triaging candidate findings rather than counting reports as completed fixes.</li></ul><h4>Track deduplication and coverage, not report volume</h4><ul><li>Repeated findings and uncertain scan coverage led to report fingerprints, evidence requirements, and explicit status transitions. Recorded clean scanned areas as well as findings. This public account describes methods only, without customer identities, internal service names, or vulnerability details.</li></ul><h4>Make test cleanup depend on ownership evidence</h4><ul><li>Used localhost binding, unique ports, task-specific directories, and ownership markers to protect shared environments. Resources are not deleted when ownership cannot be established. Cleanup runs after success, failure, or timeout, with port checks; cleanup failures are reported separately rather than replacing test outcomes. Later separated each agent’s test environment completely.</li></ul><h4>Migrate task storage without abandoning in-flight work</h4><ul><li>When moving file-based task management to PostgreSQL, took snapshots and rehearsed the schema before waiting for important in-flight work to finish. Retained migration archives and outcome records. Reused these reversible-migration lessons in the independent game’s mailbox and task systems.</li></ul><h4>Test current builds with fresh evidence and reduce operational overhead</h4><ul><li>After shared-module changes, pinned versions again, synchronized dependent repositories, and rebuilt rather than reusing stale binaries or evidence. Planned data collection before major test runs, used a fresh test-agent conversation, and added isolated analysis of the frozen version before testing.</li><li>When comprehensive pre-deployment backups became an operational burden, simplified that workflow around the last successful version and build identifier. Required a necessity check before introducing abstractions, caches, retries, or concurrency. These are context-specific workflow choices, not universal guidance to omit backups or a security guarantee.</li></ul><h4>Automate task closure while preserving change boundaries</h4><ul><li>Used isolated git worktrees and ownership markers for the task lifecycle. Incorporated new-branch pushes, evidence archiving, and cleanup into closure, while explicitly excluding force pushes, history rewrites, mainline merges, and remote-branch deletion.</li></ul><h4>In progress: isolate company and personal-project data</h4><ul><li>Work is ongoing to separate previously shared task-database areas and hooks. Changes are tested in a test database and cross-reviewed by two models; review exposed a remaining isolation-boundary issue that is still being corrected. Bringing personal-project quota optimizations back to the company workflow is a future step, not a completed outcome.</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">Based on the author’s work record updated November 3, 2026; company code, deployments, and test evidence have not been independently audited. Internal links, system-size figures, and specific vulnerability information are omitted.</p>"}, {"title": "Professional Experience", "body": "<h3>YingMan Co Ltd — Technical Director</h3><p>November 2021–Present</p><ul><li>Lead a 24-person team developing third-party sports and electronic gaming platforms, an app-based casino platform, and USDT deposit and withdrawal wallet services.</li><li>Combine technical leadership with principal engineering and software architecture responsibilities.</li></ul><h3>Paradise Games Co Ltd</h3><p>October 2019–October 2021</p><p><strong>Game Department Manager / Principal Software Engineer / Principal Software Architect</strong> | January–October 2021  </p><p><strong>Principal Software Engineer</strong> | March 2020–January 2021  </p><p><strong>Senior Staff Software Developer</strong> | October 2019–March 2020</p><ul><li>Built the Yggdrasill WebSocket framework in Go, abstracting connection handling, binary packet serialization, compression, push notifications, multicast, broadcast, and topic subscriptions for game-server developers.</li><li>Integrated message-queue communication to distribute topic messages across server instances in a cluster.</li><li>Developed JavaScript and Go client SDKs, including a Cocos frontend SDK, so teams could use the shared communication protocol without implementing transport and serialization logic for each game.</li><li>Designed a second-generation distributed wallet service and developed game-platform services for betting-record queries, RTP management, and risk controls.</li><li>Supported releases of slot, card, fishing, and regional mahjong games beginning in June 2020.</li><li>Grew the team from 4 to 55 people. Directly managed nearly 30 developers and coordinated approximately 55 people across backend, web, Cocos, and 2D/3D art, including task allocation and delivery schedules.</li></ul><h3>Zhong Ji Consulting — Senior Technical Director</h3><p>August 2018–October 2019</p><ul><li>Built an engineering team from zero to eight people, mentoring five junior developers and coordinating delivery with product managers and QA.</li><li>Led development of backend architecture and 15 poker and slot games, including scoring, jackpots, multiplayer Texas Hold’em, baccarat, RTP management, and risk controls.</li><li>Designed microservices, distributed transaction coordination, NoSQL schemas, Redis Cluster data models, and binary-protocol SDKs for game clients.</li><li>Developed Go services for gRPC communication and CPU-intensive game calculations, alongside Node.js services for I/O-heavy workloads.</li><li>Optimized a Node.js scoring implementation to complete a 10-million-iteration workload in 25–30 seconds, compared with approximately two minutes for the reference verification program, in internal testing.</li><li>Ran a 48-hour baccarat stability test with 8,000 users on an Intel Core i7-8700 machine with 16 GB RAM, with no errors reported during that test.</li><li>Established unit and integration testing, release and deployment workflows using Jenkins and shell scripts, and architecture documentation in PlantUML and Markdown.</li></ul><h3>Gama Technology — Senior Software Engineer</h3><p>November 2016–August 2018</p><ul><li>Led a four-person team and built a reusable TypeScript/Koa 2 API framework to replace a legacy C++ system while preserving the existing database schema.</li><li>Applied dependency injection and inversion of control to support extension, testing, and adoption by other engineers.</li><li>Developed services for casino administration, baccarat scoring, member management, betting records, loyalty points, rebates, first-deposit offers, and agent commissions.</li><li>Built configurable promotion logic and Android operator applications with supporting APIs.</li><li>Achieved over 90% project test coverage using Mocha, Dredd, and Newman; integrated Swagger documentation, linting, Git hooks, and Jenkins into the delivery workflow.</li><li>Created onboarding and technical documentation so additional engineers could maintain the framework and develop new features.</li></ul><h3>SYSTEX / SoftMobie — Software Engineer</h3><p>February 2015–October 2016</p><ul><li>Contributed to seven to eight client projects in teams of two to three engineers, primarily developing Android applications in Java.</li><li>Developed and maintained securities applications, including Cathay Securities applications, implementing new screens, features, and ongoing maintenance.</li><li>Worked on a stock-value application and a music application using YouTube and Spotify.</li></ul><h3>Xing Wang International (GPK) — Web Frontend Engineer</h3><p>July 2012–May 2014</p><ul><li>Converted design assets into HTML, CSS, and jQuery pages for the company website, promotions, and sporting events, coordinating integration with backend engineers.</li><li>Took on additional responsibilities in promotional planning, cross-team scheduling, and payment-provider coordination, including handling exceptional payment cases.</li></ul><h3>Restored Project & Implementation Details</h3><ul><li>Paradise Games: in addition to the game platform and second-generation wallet, delivered a platform community system. Defined a custom packet layout that converted Go objects into compressed binary packets, with the frontend SDK restoring JavaScript objects.</li><li>Zhongji: the original résumé reports approximately 80,000 ArangoDB/SSD inserts per second, 180,000 messages per second over one WebSocket, and 250,000 requests per second on one core. These are historical, differently scoped measurements, not a combined capacity claim, and have not been reverified.</li><li>Zhongji: the original account also describes using a phone to SSH into the company system, fix a server bug and deploy when no computer was available at home. This is a personal troubleshooting anecdote, not a recommended standard release process.</li><li>Gama: helped migrate a C++/Qt legacy system to a Node.js API framework while preserving the database schema. The source describes GLI compliance as a requirement, not a newly asserted independent certification.</li><li>Gama: the source records the framework continuing to support new features for gaming halls and casinos, and stable use of the operator leaderboard app at that time. Work also included another Android operations app and its API. These are historical statuses, not claims about current operation.</li></ul>"}, {"title": "Education", "body": "<p><strong>Asia University, Taiwan</strong>  </p><p>Bachelor’s degree in Optoelectronics and Communications Engineering  </p><p>September 2006–July 2010</p>"}, {"title": "Selected Work", "body": "<ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/thaiwu0107/uwsbenchmark/blob/master/README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Node.js WebSocket benchmark ↗</a><p>Node.js server example and load tests: serializes backend JSON into binary to reduce transfer cost, then restores JSON through the custom frontend SDK.</p></li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/thaiwu0107/arangodb_benchmark\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArangoDB benchmark ↗</a><p>ArangoDB NoSQL database benchmark results.</p></li><li><a href=\"https://youtu.be/sTKdw8_kR4M\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Technical documentation walkthrough ↗</a><p>Technical documentation video: a quick recorded walkthrough of the documents.</p></li><li><a href=\"https://youtu.be/EyMuzKDEhYA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Game-client SDK documentation ↗</a><p>Game frontend SDK documentation: breaks down game requirements and exposes APIs so frontend developers can focus on game visuals rather than communication details.</p></li><li><a href=\"https://youtu.be/zcpFGeCGqXo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Game demonstration ↗</a><p>Selected game demonstrations.</p></li></ul><p><a href=\"https://youtu.be/gDfycLjik1c?t=528\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Additional game demonstration (8:48)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://thaiwu0107.github.io/ThaiWu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Personal technical blog</a></p>"}, {"title": "About Me & Work Philosophy", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\">Restored from the original GitHub résumé. Chinese retains the original wording; English is a faithful translation. Historical dates, experience counts, work preferences and test figures have not been reverified or extrapolated to the present. Technical explanations reflect the author’s understanding at the time, not universal performance guarantees.</p><p>Every company is looking for the right employee, and I am also evaluating the companies that interview me. Like choosing a partner, both sides need to feel that the relationship is right.</p><p>In the original résumé, I described 12 years of software development, more than eight years in gaming, and six years in management. My experience covers slots, card games, fish games and mahjong, including architecture, risk controls, RTP management and high-concurrency systems. I enjoy sharing development experience and working as a technical manager. These experience counts are historical, not recalculated totals.</p><p>I value smooth team communication: listening to other people, understanding their perspectives with empathy, proposing solutions, and patiently guiding teammates.</p><p>I am persistent and attentive to detail. I take responsibility for completing my work, value opportunities to improve my technical skills, manage myself well, and proactively take ownership of problems.</p><p>If I have the opportunity to join a company that treats its employees generously, I will respond with the same generosity and give my best.</p><h3>Work Preferences — From the Original Résumé</h3><p>Expected salary: negotiable.</p><p>A role that requires employees to rehearse and perform for customers at company year-end events, or act as service staff, is not the kind of work I am seeking.</p><p>The original résumé requested arrangements such as two remote days per week, paid leave, or rotation between Taipei and Taichung, to be discussed with the company. These are historical preferences; their current applicability remains to be confirmed.</p><p>In the original résumé, I stated that I would cooperate with colleagues during typhoon-related closures rather than insist on time off, take responsibility for completing my work on time, and accommodate overtime according to project needs.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Family and health details are omitted. These are statements from the original résumé, not a waiver of statutory leave or workplace safety protections.</p>"}, {"title": "Learning & Career Transition", "body": "<p>When I left GPK, I decided to rebuild my technical foundations. I combined self-study with six months of courses at UCOM. Although that was not equivalent to a four-year university program, I studied seriously in class and regularly read original-language materials late into the night, using translation tools to strengthen my fundamentals.</p><p>My curiosity makes me want to get to the bottom of how things work. At the time, I slept very little and even considered returning to university for a four-year computer science degree at a national university. My instructor advised me to gain professional experience first and consider a part-time graduate program later. After completing the courses, I successfully joined the Android development department at Systex and took on that challenge.</p><p>These experiences are evidence of my passion for programming and my ability to teach myself. They underpin both my confidence and the way I work.</p>"}, {"title": "Engineering Reflections & Practice", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\">Restored from the original GitHub résumé. Chinese retains the original wording; English is a faithful translation. Historical dates, experience counts, work preferences and test figures have not been reverified or extrapolated to the present. Technical explanations reflect the author’s understanding at the time, not universal performance guarantees.</p><h3>Zhongji Consulting — Technical Director (5–8 people), August 2018–October 2019</h3><p>I joined at the start of the company. Within a year, we went from having no games or backend architecture to completing 15 games, including slots and baccarat. The first four months were spent recruiting and getting the team in place, leaving a little more than six months of actual development.</p><p>I led the backend team in building a microservice-based game server architecture for gaming products: slot scoring, jackpots, multiplayer Texas Hold’em, multiplayer baccarat, risk controls and RTP management.</p><p>Because the company had no DBA, I designed the NoSQL schemas and the Redis Cluster key and data models.</p><p>My original explanation described Node.js as using a single-threaded event loop, with asynchronous I/O handled through underlying C++ and epoll mechanisms. This is a historical description of my reasoning, not a precise claim that every I/O operation uses a C++ worker thread.</p><p>The probability engineer provided algorithm rules and a Go verification program. After receiving the reel tables, we reimplemented and optimized the scoring logic in Node.js. In the recorded test, ten million runs took about two minutes in the reference program and 25–30 seconds in the optimized implementation, exceeding 400,000 runs per second. My focus was the CPU-heavy core scoring algorithm, which could otherwise become the bottleneck. This compares specific implementations, not the inherent speed of Go and Node.js.</p><p>The original résumé records a Node.js WebSocket implementation with C/C++ bindings supporting one million live connections in one process using approximately 500 MB of memory. The exact workload and measurement conditions are not documented here; this is a historical reported result, not a general capacity promise.</p><p>A slot workload test included Redis access and writing bet records to the database. The original record describes one Node.js process supporting 2,000 connections with one spin per second.</p><p>I described using PM2 to run 12 clustered processes for load balancing on a Linux desktop, and estimated a capacity of 2,000 × 12 concurrent users. I identified Redis as a potential bottleneck requiring clustering and partitioning into services. That multiplication is a historical capacity estimate, not proof of a separately verified 24,000-user end-to-end test.</p><p>The CTO had previously worked as a technical manager on a Google Cluster project, according to my original account. I felt fortunate to learn from him, particularly the principles and judgment involved in splitting microservices.</p><h3>Gama Technology — Software Engineer (4 people)</h3><p>The original retrospective gives May 2017–November 2018, while the employment table gives November 2016–August 2018. This inconsistency is retained for clarification rather than silently resolved.</p><p>Gama was a subsidiary of Jumbo Technology. I led an API server framework project that rewrote a legacy C++ system using TypeScript and Koa 2.</p><p>I used IoC and dependency injection to design the API server framework. Unit tests used Mocha; documentation-driven integration tests used Dredd and Newman; API documentation used Swagger. Jenkins ran automated tests after Git commits.</p><p>I used JSLint and TSLint during development and wrote tests alongside features. The original project reported more than 90% test coverage. A reusable framework made mocks and test code faster to write.</p><p>When I completed an API, I also updated its Swagger documentation. Git hooks ran the checks at commit time to ensure the code behaved as expected.</p><p>I required test coverage above 80% before a commit could proceed. This first gate pushed me to write the tests that would later provide a safety net for refactoring.</p><p>After integrating with the frontend, Jenkins ran end-to-end tests to check that backend-only changes did not break unchanged frontend behavior.</p><p>Load tests also had to meet requirements before a release package was built. At that time, I still needed a Docker-based way to restore the test database; I later implemented that part while working at Zhongji.</p><p>Whenever a bug or QA report arrived, I added a test for that scenario to prevent recurrence. I used Jira for work tracking and Git issues, branches and merges to manage changes.</p><p>Before joining Gama, I was not familiar with JavaScript. As the original résumé jokes, Java and JavaScript are related like a dog and a hot dog. I spent roughly a month fixing bugs, then another month moving into feature development, before building new projects.</p><p>I later switched to TypeScript and became productive in about two weeks, developing a framework that teammates could use for new API servers. I do not resist changing languages when the work requires it.</p><h3>Systex / SoftMobie — Software Engineer, February 2015–October 2016</h3><p>I worked there from 2015 until the end of October 2016. Most projects were securities-related, with some music applications. As it was a client-services company, I worked on the projects assigned by my manager.</p><h3>GPK — Web Engineer & Customer Campaign Planning</h3><p>My second job was at an online gaming company, initially developing frontend pages with HTML and CSS.</p><p>Designers supplied the visual templates. I implemented the frontend layouts and passed them to backend engineers for integration, mainly for campaign pages, the company homepage, online sports weekly features and gaming-related content.</p><p>After colleagues left, I also took on campaign planning and assisted with cross-department staffing coordination and product schedules, becoming a point of contact across departments.</p><p>In addition to building pages, I coordinated schedules and communication. Website changes were mainly needed around holidays or major sporting events, so I was taught additional responsibilities during quieter periods. I was happy to learn more about gaming operations and campaign planning.</p><p>When a payment department manager was preparing to leave, our good working relationship and his trust in me led him to teach me the application processes, exception-handling procedures and liaison work for third-party payment providers in mainland China.</p><p>I later handled payment applications, customer-service exceptions and communication with those providers. These were unfamiliar areas, and I was happy to accept the tasks and learn through hands-on work.</p><p><a href=\"https://thaiwu0107.github.io/ThaiWu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Personal blog</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/thaiwu0107\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub</a></p>"}], "zh": [{"title": "核心技能", "body": "<ul><li><strong>程式語言：</strong> Go、TypeScript、JavaScript、SQL、AQL、Shell；具 Android Java 開發經驗。</li><li><strong>後端架構：</strong> WebSocket、REST API、gRPC、微服務、分散式系統、高可用、非同步 I/O、並行處理、IoC 與 DI。</li><li><strong>資料與訊息：</strong> ArangoDB、Redis Cluster、MySQL、Microsoft SQL Server、NATS、JetStream。</li><li><strong>測試與交付：</strong> Docker、Jenkins、Git、Nginx、PM2、Mocha、Dredd、Newman、Swagger、Jira、PlantUML、Markdown。</li><li><strong>AI 協作與專案工具：</strong> 多代理調度、Claude Code、OpenAI Codex、Gemini CLI、MCP、模型分配、上下文管理、Python、PostgreSQL、Godot 4。</li><li><strong>領域經驗：</strong> 遊戲平台、賭場管理、錢包服務、注單查詢、RTP 管理與風險控制。</li></ul><p><strong>原技能表補充：</strong>演算法、資料結構、Epoll／Reactor、Multithreading、Multiprocess、Coroutine、Web Server／Application Development 與 Game Server。</p>"}, {"title": "目前獨立專案經驗", "body": "<h3>獨立遊戲開發｜獨立開發者與 AI 系統設計</h3><p>2026 年 9 月至今｜Godot 4 第三人稱末日生存遊戲，開發中</p><p>設計並指揮多 AI 代理協作系統，負責架構設計、任務分工、審查標準、驗收與迭代決策；程式主要由 AI 代理撰寫。遊戲尚未發行。</p><h4>角色與架構：把 AI 協作設計成工程團隊</h4><ul><li>設計三個 Claude 長期角色，分別負責流程與成本、內容產線派工，以及伺服器與遊戲系統；搭配三個 Codex 執行帳號及 Gemini CLI，分離決策、實作與審查責任。架構在一個月內歷經五次以上迭代，從單一主對話演進為分權協作。</li><li>Codex 執行層設定每帳號最多 15 條並行對話；這是設定容量，不代表持續滿載或已驗證的吞吐量。本人主導架構、分工規則、驗收與決策，AI 代理撰寫大部分程式。</li></ul><h4>端到端派工與可追蹤交付</h4><ul><li>將方向拆成大項、可驗收中項與執行小項（G／M／W），工單經統一入口依剩餘額度、任務難度選擇帳號、模型與推理強度，每次派工綁回需求並自動建立小項。</li><li>設計「派工 → 初檢 → 獨立複審 → 安裝 → 多輪驗證 → 狀態回寫」流程，以資料庫驅動進度網頁，需人工判斷的事項另行呈現。2026/11/03 紀錄含 31 個大項、401 個中項，其中 197 個標記完成；自 10/27 起約 2,100 筆 Codex 派工是活動紀錄，不等同完成需求數。</li></ul><h4>Go 調度中樞與漸進遷移</h4><ul><li>主導 AI 輔助開發單一 Go 調度服務，逐步整併上百支常駐 Python 腳本。服務涵蓋事件 hook 派送、Codex app-server 對話驅動、派工狀態機、信箱、排程、健康檢查、子程序監督與記憶體上限。</li><li>以舊版輸入輸出建立黃金樣本，進行 Go／Python 對照測試，採三階段可回退切換。依專案統計，服務有 35 個內部套件、約 131,000 行非測試程式及 410 個測試檔；主體 48 個中項已完成，其餘腳本持續遷移。程式量與測試檔數不代表覆蓋率或全數通過。</li></ul><h4>跨代理通訊與長時間工作連續性</h4><ul><li>以 PostgreSQL 角色信箱取代檔案信箱，建立收件、未取件告警及喚醒流程；急件與故障使用備援通道，減少直接訊息打斷正在執行的工作。工具呼叫及失敗寫入資料庫，保留可回查軌跡。</li><li>設計對話自動壓縮、滾動交接檔與主角色 A／B 換手，保留任務編號、檔案位置與待回覆事項，並以資料庫避免兩個主角色同時生效。建立重開機後恢復應用程式、資料庫、排程與信箱的清單；目標為長時間運作，不宣稱已達成特定可用率。</li></ul><h4>品質關卡、故障偵測與驗收</h4><ul><li>Gemini 進行第一輪唯讀檢查，結果需抽查；Claude Opus 與 Codex 分別獨立複審，雙方及自測通過後才整合。第三輪起聚焦重大問題，避免低價值反覆修改；持續未過時改由簡化方案裁定。</li><li>針對無進展回合設計偵測、中斷及重啟流程；同一錯誤第三次出現時要求換方法。工具前後 hook 約束角色職責、掃描範圍與測試方式；定期保養檢查把新故障轉為後續檢查項。</li><li>Godot 測試採排隊、資源預檢與無視窗自動測試；效能須在連接伺服器條件下量測。無視窗通過不當作視覺驗收，角色動作、伺服器連線版與 60 FPS 目標仍在進行。</li></ul><h4>以量化資料改善模型與額度分配</h4><ul><li>分析 541 筆派工的帳號額度差，估計高階、中高階、中階模型每件分別約耗 6.2%、1.1%、0.2% 週額度；只作量級比較，並行工作可能重複歸因，並非精確單件 token 或帳單成本。</li><li>據此將高階模型保留給首次打通的難題，審查重用原對話，小修改使用較輕量模型。以無需 LLM 的腳本依額度與重置時間切換全速、普通、超省三檔，搭配低額度停工、交接與恢復流程。高階用量降低 70–80% 是預估，尚非實測節省或產能保證。</li></ul><h4>知識工程與 AI 內容產線</h4><ul><li>建立「規則索引 → 主題檔 → 證據」三層知識結構、十餘份主題知識庫、47 個 Claude skill、約 90 條專案規則與向量記憶，讓重複派工、審查、盤點與交接成為可重用流程。</li><li>整合 NVIDIA Kimodo 動作生成與標準骨架、MotionBricks 轉場烘焙、Tripo 3D、Blender MCP、ComfyUI、Gemini 生圖及 ElevenLabs 音效；建立面數、UV、開放邊、色板等機器可檢查標準。</li><li>專案紀錄列出約 850 張環境貼圖與 307 件音效已完成審查與分流，另整理建築尺寸、怪物及數值檢查規格。素材經審查不代表全部已上線或整體遊戲已完成。</li></ul><h3>判斷與執行調整：從瓶頸到下一次驗證</h3><h4>先界定問題，再擴大協作｜9 月中至 10 月初</h4><ul><li>最初用一個 Claude 協調、一個 Codex 實作，以 Markdown 檔交換訊息。隨文件累積，重讀成本增加，換對話也容易遺失進度；判斷瓶頸不只是模型能力，而是資訊保存與交接方式。先限制未結訊息與文件大小，再逐步重設通訊流程。</li></ul><h4>把知識和責任分開｜10 月 11–20 日</h4><ul><li>規則分散、代理讀錯文件後，將知識改為「索引 → 主題檔 → 證據」，並把錯誤轉成可重用規則。觀察到初檢產出可能缺乏可靠證據，便限制為唯讀、抽查後採用，不把模型回覆直接視為驗證。</li><li>單一對話同時派工、審查、管流程，造成上下文膨脹與自審風險；改為三角色分權，拆開流程管理、執行派工與系統審查。A／B 換手在專案紀錄中通過兩輪測試，但之後發現清空對話會遺失進行中的子代理工作，再補上「先交件或交接」及 hook 阻擋。</li></ul><h4>從提醒代理，改為可追蹤的系統｜10 月 22–25 日</h4><ul><li>檔案信箱與直接工具傳話不穩定，改以背景送話程序及 PostgreSQL 信箱處理，加入收件確認、未取件警告。停止讓備用對話持續等待收件，並建立重開機恢復清單，在工作連續性與額度消耗間取捨。</li></ul><h4>記錄之後才優化｜10 月 27–30 日</h4><ul><li>開始將每筆派工存入資料庫，再分析執行與用量。大型美術投票審查太慢、太貴，改為作者自檢及初步檢查；程式整合仍保留獨立交叉審查。把已決定的計畫立即轉成有負責人的待辦，而非只留在文件中。</li></ul><h4>遷移失敗後縮小切換單位｜10 月 31 日至 11 月 3 日</h4><ul><li>常駐 Python 腳本過多，記憶體與追錯成本上升，因此主導以 Go 服務收斂。先保存舊版輸入輸出作黃金樣本，做行為對照及分階段回退；專案紀錄描述數次安裝失敗，包括一次記憶體耗盡，且退回舊版。</li><li>依失敗調整為一次只切換一支、每支觀察 10 分鐘，有問題的個別保留舊版，避免把「一次全面成功」當目標。這是來源描述的回退經驗，未獨立驗證零中斷或正式環境可用率。</li></ul><h4>用實測校正審查，用限制終止低價值循環</h4><ul><li>審查曾判定不受影響，但實測出現三項失敗；因此把實機結果與寫入後讀回置於模型判斷之上。UI 改用實機截圖判斷，效能要求連接伺服器量測；無視窗測試不代表視覺通過。</li><li>單件曾累積多達 25 張審查工單，便讓第三輪起只處理重大問題，持續未過時提交簡化方案。同一錯誤反覆出現時要求換方法，並刪減非必要抽象，把投入拉回可驗收的核心功能。</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">資料範圍：依 2026/11/03 更新的本人專案紀錄整理；統計由來源提供，未另行查驗資料庫、原始碼或測試證據。遊戲仍在開發，以上流程與數據不構成已發行產品的可靠度或效能承諾。</p>"}, {"title": "目前工作案例：AI 輔助後端工程", "body": "<h3>既有線上遊戲平台｜AI 輔助後端工程流程</h3><p>2026 年 7 月底至今｜公司工作案例，匿名整理</p><p>本人負責流程設計、邊界判斷、修復與驗證安排；AI 參與調查、程式實作及測試。以下描述個人方法與迭代，不等同獨立完成所有程式，亦不代表正式環境修復均已驗收。</p><h4>先畫系統地圖，再允許修改</h4><ul><li>2026 年 7 月底起，為既有線上遊戲平台設計 AI 輔助缺陷分析、修復、本機測試及開發環境部署流程。面對多個服務與歷史 repo，以實際建置與部署設定判定有效系統範圍；要求修改前核對遠端、版本及未提交變更，並使用獨立分支。</li></ul><h4>按任務載入知識，分開發現與修復</h4><ul><li>大型規範反覆載入增加上下文成本，因此建立「任務 → 必讀文件」路由及各代理的入口文件。將唯讀問題調查與修復決策分工：候選報告先經分流，不把發現數直接當成修復數。</li></ul><h4>補上去重與掃描覆蓋，而非追求報告量</h4><ul><li>重複問題及未知掃描範圍暴露管理缺口，因而為報告加入指紋去重、狀態流轉及證據要求；同時記錄已掃描但未發現問題的範圍。履歷僅描述匿名方法，不揭露客戶、內部服務或漏洞細節。</li></ul><h4>讓測試清理以擁有權證據為前提</h4><ul><li>為避免測試影響共用環境，採本機綁定、唯一埠號、任務專屬目錄與擁有權標記；無法證明資源屬於本次測試就不刪除。成功、失敗及逾時都要執行清理並檢查埠，清理失敗獨立回報，不覆蓋測試結果。後續再將不同代理的測試環境完全分開。</li></ul><h4>任務資料庫切換：先等在途工作結束</h4><ul><li>從檔案任務管理轉向 PostgreSQL 時，避免中斷重要工作；先快照與 schema 演練，等在途任務自然結束，再切換並保存封存及結果紀錄。將可復原的遷移經驗延伸到個人遊戲的信箱與待辦系統。</li></ul><h4>用新證據測新版本，並簡化操作負擔</h4><ul><li>共用模組變更後重新固定版本、同步相依 repo 並重建，不沿用舊執行檔或舊測試證據。測試前先規劃同輪要收的資料，由新對話的測試角色執行，並先對凍結版本做隔離分析。</li><li>發現部署前全套備份本身成為負擔後，改以最後成功版本與建置編號作回退依據；新增抽象、快取、重試或併發機制前先檢查必要性。這些是特定工作流程中的調整，並非通用免備份或安全保證。</li></ul><h4>把交付收尾自動化，仍保留變更邊界</h4><ul><li>以隔離 git worktree 與擁有權標記管理任務生命週期，將新分支推送、證據歸檔與清理納入收尾；明確排除強推、改寫歷史、合併主幹及刪除遠端分支。</li></ul><h4>尚在進行：公司與個人專案的資料隔離</h4><ul><li>正在將曾共用的任務資料庫與 hook 分隔；先在測試庫驗證並做雙模型交叉審查。審查已發現隔離尚有邊界問題，仍在修正，不能列為完成。個人專案的額度優化方法回饋公司流程亦屬後續計畫。</li></ul><p class=\"source-note\">依本人 2026/11/03 更新的工作紀錄整理，未獨立稽核公司原始碼、部署或測試證據；不公開內部連結、規模數字與具體漏洞資訊。</p>"}, {"title": "工作經歷", "body": "<h3>盈滿有限公司｜技術總監</h3><p>2021 年 11 月至今</p><ul><li>帶領 24 人團隊開發第三方體育平台、電子遊戲平台、APP 娛樂城平台及 USDT 轉入轉出錢包服務。</li><li>結合技術管理、Principal Software Engineer 與軟體架構職責，推動平台開發。</li></ul><h3>天堂遊戲有限公司</h3><p>2019 年 10 月至 2021 年 10 月</p><p><strong>遊戲部經理／Principal Software Engineer／Principal Software Architect</strong>｜2021 年 1–10 月  </p><p><strong>Principal Software Engineer</strong>｜2020 年 3 月至 2021 年 1 月  </p><p><strong>Senior Staff Software Developer</strong>｜2019 年 10 月至 2020 年 3 月</p><ul><li>以 Go 建置 Yggdrasill WebSocket 框架，封裝連線、二進位封包序列化、壓縮、主動推播、多播、廣播與主題訂閱，降低遊戲開發者處理底層通訊的負擔。</li><li>整合訊息佇列，支援叢集中不同伺服器間的主題訊息傳遞。</li><li>開發 JavaScript、Go Client SDK 及 Cocos 前端 SDK，統一通訊協定與資料轉換流程。</li><li>設計第二代分散式錢包服務，開發注單查詢、RTP 管理與風控系統。</li><li>支援老虎機、棋牌、魚機及多種地方麻將遊戲，自 2020 年 6 月起陸續上線。</li><li>將團隊從 4 人擴展至 55 人，直接管理近 30 位工程師，協調遊戲後端、Web、Cocos 與 2D／3D 美術的分工及交付時程。</li></ul><h3>中極顧問｜技術主任</h3><p>2018 年 8 月至 2019 年 10 月</p><ul><li>從零建立 8 人研發團隊，培訓 5 位初階工程師，與產品及 QA 協調開發交付。</li><li>帶領後端架構及 15 款撲克、老虎機遊戲開發，涵蓋計分、Jackpot、多人德州撲克、百家樂、RTP 與風險控制。</li><li>設計微服務、分散式交易協調、NoSQL schema、Redis Cluster 資料模型與二進位遊戲 SDK。</li><li>以 Go 開發 gRPC 與 CPU 密集型計算服務，搭配 Node.js 處理 I/O 密集型工作。</li><li>優化 Node.js 計分實作，在內部測試中將一千萬次執行時間縮短至 25–30 秒；對照驗證程式約需兩分鐘。</li><li>於 Intel Core i7-8700、16 GB RAM 主機進行百家樂 8,000 使用者、48 小時穩定性測試，該測試期間未出現錯誤。</li><li>以 Jenkins、Shell、PlantUML 與 Markdown 建立測試、版本交付、部署流程及架構文件。</li></ul><h3>嘉碼科技｜資深軟體工程師</h3><p>2016 年 11 月至 2018 年 8 月</p><ul><li>帶領 4 人團隊，以 TypeScript／Koa 2 建立可重用 API 框架，改寫原 C++ 系統並保留既有資料庫 schema。</li><li>運用 IoC、DI 設計可擴充、可測試架構，培訓團隊以共用框架開發新功能。</li><li>開發賭場管理、百家樂計分、會員、注單、忠誠點數、反水、首存優惠與代理抽成服務。</li><li>建立可依平台需求設定的優惠邏輯，以及現場操作人員使用的 Android APP 與 API。</li><li>使用 Mocha、Dredd、Newman 達成超過 90% 專案測試涵蓋率，整合 Swagger、lint、Git hooks 與 Jenkins。</li><li>撰寫培訓與技術文件，支援後續工程師維護框架及擴充功能。</li></ul><h3>精誠資訊／精誠隨想 SoftMobie｜軟體工程師</h3><p>2015 年 2 月至 2016 年 10 月</p><ul><li>於 2–3 人團隊參與 7–8 個客戶專案，主要以 Java 開發 Android 應用。</li><li>開發及維護證券相關 APP，包含國泰證券的新畫面、功能與後續維護。</li><li>參與台股價值及使用 YouTube、Spotify 的音樂應用開發。</li></ul><h3>興旺國際 GPK｜網頁前端工程師</h3><p>2012 年 7 月至 2014 年 5 月</p><ul><li>將美術設計轉為 HTML、CSS、jQuery 頁面，製作官網、活動與運動賽事頁面，與後端協作整合。</li><li>兼任活動企劃、跨部門時程協調與支付供應商溝通，處理異常付款案件。</li></ul><h3>原履歷補充：專案與實作細節</h3><ul><li>天堂遊戲：除遊戲平台與第二代錢包外，也完成包網平台社群系統；制定專用 packet layout，讓 Go object 自動轉為壓縮二進位封包，再由前端 SDK 還原為 JavaScript object。</li><li>中極顧問：原履歷記錄 ArangoDB／SSD 每秒新增約 8 萬筆、一條 WebSocket 每秒處理約 18 萬則訊息、單核心約 25 萬 req。這些為不同測試口徑的歷史自述，不合併成同一項系統容量，也未重新驗證。</li><li>中極顧問：原文亦分享，曾在未帶電腦回家時，用手機 SSH 連回公司修復 server bug 並上版。這是個人應變經驗，不作為建議的標準發布流程。</li><li>嘉碼科技：協助 C++／Qt 舊系統轉為 Node.js API 框架而不更動資料庫 schema；原文說明以符合 GLI 標準為要求，並非新增獨立認證聲明。</li><li>嘉碼科技：原履歷記錄當時的框架持續支援娛樂廳／賭場新功能，龍虎榜 operator APP 穩定使用；亦開發打線 Android APP 與 API。這些是當時狀態，不宣稱目前仍在運行。</li></ul>"}, {"title": "學歷", "body": "<p><strong>亞洲大學｜光電通訊系學士</strong>  </p><p>2006 年 9 月至 2010 年 7 月</p>"}, {"title": "作品參考", "body": "<ul><li><a href=\"https://github.com/thaiwu0107/uwsbenchmark/blob/master/README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Node.js WebSocket 壓測 ↗</a><p>Node.js server 範例與壓測：後端將 JSON 轉成 binary 降低傳輸成本，前端以自製 SDK 還原 JSON。</p></li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/thaiwu0107/arangodb_benchmark\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArangoDB 壓測 ↗</a><p>ArangoDB NoSQL 資料庫壓測數據。</p></li><li><a href=\"https://youtu.be/sTKdw8_kR4M\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">技術文件展示 ↗</a><p>技術文件撰寫影片：以錄影快速展示文件內容。</p></li><li><a href=\"https://youtu.be/EyMuzKDEhYA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">遊戲前端 SDK 文件 ↗</a><p>遊戲前端 SDK 文件：拆解遊戲需求，以 API 封裝通訊，讓前端專注在遊戲畫面開發。</p></li><li><a href=\"https://youtu.be/zcpFGeCGqXo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">遊戲作品展示 ↗</a><p>部分遊戲作品展示。</p></li></ul><p><a href=\"https://youtu.be/gDfycLjik1c?t=528\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">另一支遊戲作品影片（8:48）</a></p><p><a href=\"https://thaiwu0107.github.io/ThaiWu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">個人技術部落格</a></p>"}, {"title": "關於我與工作理念", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\">以下補回原始 GitHub 履歷的自我介紹與工作心得。中文保留原話，英文為忠實翻譯；舊任期、年資、工作安排與測試數字屬當時紀錄，未重新驗證或推算成目前狀態。原文技術說明為當時的個人理解，不作通用效能保證。</p><p>每家公司在物色適合自己的員工，同時我也在物色面試我的公司 就像交女友一樣彼此要互相滿意才能交往。</p><p>12年軟體開發，博奕經驗8年多，6年管理經驗，熟悉老虎機、棋牌類、魚機、麻將類遊戲開發、架構設計、風險控制、RTP控制，處理高併發經驗豐富，喜歡分享開發經驗、擔任技術經理。</p><p>團隊溝通順暢（注重人和，能傾聽他人想法、理解同理並提出解決辦法，細心指導)</p><p>耐力佳，注動細節，細心，對於所負職責皆能盡心盡力完成，珍惜工作及技術上磨練，自我管理佳，能擔責並處理解決，主動性高。</p><p>有幸能加入對員工慷慨的公司，會同樣慷慨回報，全力以赴。</p><h3>工作期待（原履歷紀錄）</h3><p>期望薪資：面議。</p><p>公司尾牙如果需要員工排演，表演給客戶看，或是充當服務人員，不是我所希望的工作。</p><p>原履歷曾提出每週兩天遠端、全薪假或台北／台中輪調的工作安排，希望與公司協調；這是舊履歷中的需求，目前適用情況仍待確認。</p><p>遇颱風假我可以配合公司同事正常工作，不會硬拗放假，責任在我，應該做的皆會於時間內完成，因應專案狀況配合加班。</p><p class=\"source-note\">工作安排已省略家庭成員與健康細節；上述為原履歷自述，不代表放棄依法享有的休假或安全保障。</p>"}, {"title": "自學與轉職歷程", "body": "<p>興旺國際在職時期，當時提出離職，是決定砍掉重練技術，靠著我自學加上上課的輔助，至恆逸上課半年，雖比不過大學四年，但是這濃縮的課，除上課認真外，下課之後我半夜經常看原文自己靠翻譯爬文去增加自己基礎。</p><p>因個性是打破砂鍋問到底，非常好奇，想知道原理，因此每天睡眠時間都非常的少，原本上課到一半還有衝動想回去重考大學四年，考過國立資工系。但在老師建議下選擇優先進入職場磨練，再去考國立在職碩班，也因此結束課程後，成功進入精誠資訊的 android 開發部門，接受挑戰。</p><p>對寫程式的熱忱與自學能力是這些經歷佐證了，我的自信與行為。</p>"}, {"title": "工作心得與工程實踐", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\">以下補回原始 GitHub 履歷的自我介紹與工作心得。中文保留原話，英文為忠實翻譯；舊任期、年資、工作安排與測試數字屬當時紀錄，未重新驗證或推算成目前狀態。原文技術說明為當時的個人理解，不作通用效能保證。</p><h3>中極顧問- 技術主任  （管理５－８人）  2018 / 08 ~ 2019 / 10</h3><p>從公司新創一開始就進來了，一年內從沒有任何遊戲跟後端架構到完成15款遊戲，包含老虎機，百家樂等等，前四個月人員都還未定位招募時間，實際開發時間只有半年多一點。</p><p>帶團隊製作博弈遊戲  Game  Server  Micro  Service  架構，主要是帶領後端工程師開發博弈遊戲，老虎機算分計分，Jackpot，多人對戰德州撲克，多人開獎百家樂等等算分，風險控制，RTP 控制。</p><p>因為公司沒有 DBA，我從 nosql db schema 到 redis cluster data key data modeling 的設計。</p><p>Node 雖然是單線程處理 event loop 但是底層遇到 IO 事件，自動使用 C++ 來處理多線程的異步 IO epoll 數學模型。</p><p>機率工程師給演算法規則，他會用  golang  來寫驗證程式，不過我們收到滾輪表之後，會用 nodejs 重寫一遍計分程式，優化演算法，golang 的驗證程式執行一千萬次要兩分鐘，優化過後的 nodejs 演算法只需要25秒到30秒，一秒可以執行40多萬次，核心計分演算法是整個程式核心，cpu 最吃重的地方，但是往往卻沒有真正好的演算法工程師來做優化，導致整個系統卡在這裡。</p><p>在 websocket 上使用c/c++ binding 的 nodejs 版本，一個 process 可以承受百萬 alive websockets 連線只吃 500mb 記憶體。</p><p>壓測老虎機連線包含 redis 跟寫入 db 注單，一個 nodejs process 可以每秒承受2000連線，每秒 spin 一次。</p><p>正常一台桌機Linux  i7  16核心  16g記憶體使用  pm2  開12個process  cluster  做  load  balancing  可以承受  2000  *  12的同時上線服務人數，瓶頸會在  redis  需要做  cluster  跟微服務化分區服務等等。</p><p>中極 CTO 是前 Google Cluster 項目技術經理，很幸運從他身上學到很多、微服務切割的原則與判斷原則。</p><h3>嘉碼科技- 軟體工程師(管理４人)   2017 / 05 ~ 2018 / 11</h3><p>尊博科技分公司，嘉碼科技是博弈公司 目前主導一個 API Server 框架開發的案子， 主要重寫本來舊的案子從 C++ 現在改成 Typescript+KOA2。</p><p>有用 ioc，di 等等框架來設計這個 api server，單元測試就是用摩卡，文件整合測試用了兩種dredd 跟 newman，api 文件就用 swagger 來撰寫，自動化建置就用 Jenkins 來幫忙處理 git commit 之後自己跑測試。</p><p>平常開發都會用 jslint、tslint 通常寫完一個功能測試也是寫好的(目前專案測試涵蓋率是90％以上) 有用通用架構，加速寫mock跟測試代碼的效率。</p><p>api 開發完也會補上 swagger 文件的異動，因為我有使用 git hook，當我 commit 時，都會自動執行這兩種測試確保代碼符合我的預期運行。</p><p>當然還有要求測試涵蓋率必須高於80％才能 commit， 不然第一關 commit 就先逼自己要多寫這些測試， 也是未來重構等等的基礎測試保證。</p><p>e2e 測試就等跟前端整合之後， 在利用 jenkins 來保證，前端不變的情況下， 只有後端的修正不影響前端，且保證符合預期。</p><p>還有壓力測試符合需求，以上測試都成功就建一個專案包，還缺 docker 的技術來保證還原測試資料庫、在中極服務時，以實現這段。</p><p>每次有任何 bug 或是 QA 回報，都會把情境測試補上，確保不會發生重複的 bug，自我要求代碼品質。 平常單子就用公司正在用的 jira 來管理跟 git 上開單處理分支的問題還有合併等等。</p><p>懂 java 跟 javasceipt 的都知道 java 跟 javascript 就是狗跟熱狗一樣的關係 進入這家公司前，我對 javascript 不熟。 所以大概花了一個月時間先做 bug 維護，再一個月後開始開發新功能，最後寫新案子。</p><p>甚至後面又轉成 typesceipt 大概兩個禮拜就上手開始開發框架給組員來使用開發新的 API Server 對於我來說語言的轉換我是不會排斥。</p><h3>精誠資訊-精誠隨想 SoftMobie - 軟體工程師  2015 / 02 ~ 2016 / 10</h3><p>從2015年做到2016年十月底</p><p>做過的案子大部分都是證劵相關，也有音樂類型的，畢竟是屬於接案公司，所以主管交付什麼案子就做什麼了。</p><p>我的 blog:</p><p>https://thaiwu0107.github.io/ThaiWu/</p><p>我的 github:</p><p>https://github.com/thaiwu0107</p><h3>興旺國際(GPK 最早起源的母公司) 網頁工程師兼客戶活動企劃</h3><p>第二份工作是在一家線上博弈公司一開始應徵網頁前端 HTML 與 CSS 的開發。</p><p>美工是由美工人員提交給我版模，我負責製作成前端網頁樣式，再交給後端人員處理，主要製作活動頁面與公司首頁還有線上運動賽事周刊與博弈相關的製作。</p><p>後來因為一些因素有人離職，我就順勢接了活動企劃與管理各部門的人員工作與產品時程控管助理，變成了一個各部門的窗口。</p><p>除了製作網頁外,還負責協調各部門的時程與溝通 主要是因為網頁的製作只有在過年過節或是大型球賽才會有新的活動才需要改版，平常就沒有網頁可以做就將這些事務交給我順便教我學習，我也很高興能夠學習更多博弈相關事務跟活動規劃等等博弈相關知識。</p><p>後來有個金流部門的主管要離職，因為平常關係良好又很信任我，他就將與大陸第三方金流的申請流程與處理異常程序還有金流的一些務業窗口等等事務都教會我後才離職。</p><p>爾後來我也負責公司的金流申請與客服部，異常問題也是交由我處理還有與大陸第三方金流溝通的技巧，都是我沒接觸過的領域，我也非常開心答應接下這些任務從中摸索與學習。</p>"}]};
